From: Dallas Delaney <dadelan@microsoft.com>
Subject: [PATCH] erofs: precomputed dm-verity artifacts

Squash of two commits on the ACL containerd branch:

  erofs: consume signed precomputed EROFS and dm-verity artifacts
  erofs: select the newest precomputed bundle and fail closed

Materializes signed, prebuilt EROFS blobs and Merkle trees from OCI
referrers instead of formatting a hash tree per node. Bundles are bound to
their source layer through the EROFS superblock UUID and verified against
the declared root hash; anything inconsistent is a hard error rather than
a silent fall back to local formatting. When several bundles exist the
newest by OCI creation time wins, and a malformed newest bundle fails
rather than falling back to an older one.

Layer signatures are passed to the kernel whenever they are present and
the feature is enabled. containerd does not inspect IPE policy to decide
this; the kernel alone interprets IPE.

Security corrections folded in (review of the ACL series):
  - Validate referrer descriptors before use. Descriptors come back from a
    registry's referrers response verbatim -- FetchReferrers returns
    index.Manifests unchecked and dockerFetcher.Fetch is lazy -- so a crafted
    digest reached go-digest's Digest.Verifier(), which panics rather than
    returning an error, crashing containerd from a remote input. Digest is
    now validated and the attacker-controlled Size is bounded by
    maxManifestSize before io.ReadAll allocates on the strength of it.
  - Ignore precomputed artifact annotations when dm-verity is disabled
    instead of failing the pull. Those annotations can arrive straight off an
    image manifest with no referrer discovery involved, so erroring let any
    registry make a node that never opted into dm-verity refuse an image.
    The layer is now converted the ordinary way.
  - Extend signature enforcement to native EROFS layers, alongside the
    hash-tree formatting added for them in Patch8. writeLayerSignature is
    introduced by this patch, which is why the two halves are split.

